Denote by (\alpha) this part:
$\alpha = \frac{1}{1 - z} = \frac{1+z}{(1-z)(1+z)} = \frac{1+z}{\,\, 1\, -\, z^{2}\,\, }$
Because $z$ is a nonreal complex number, then $z\ne\frac12$, therefore, \begin{align*} &\alpha = \frac{1+z}{1-z^{2}}\\ =& \frac{1}{1-z^{2}}\cdot \frac{1-z}{1-z} + \frac{z}{1-z^{2}}\cdot \frac{1-z}{1-z} \\ =& \frac{1-z}{(1-z) (1+z)(1-z)} + \frac{z}{(1-z)(1+z)}\cdot\frac{1-z}{1-z} \\ =& \frac1{1-z^{2}}-\frac{1}{(1-z)(1+z)}=\frac1{1-z^{2}}-\frac{1}{1-z^{2}} = 0. \end{align*}
Note that the latter part is a proof by contradiction, use direct proof as follows:
\begin{align}
&\left(\frac{1}{1-z} - \frac{1}{1+\bar{z}}\right)(1-z)(1+\bar{z})\ne0 \\ \implies  
&\frac{(1+\bar{z})-(1-z)}{1+\bar{z}}\ne0 \\ \implies  
&\bar{z}\ne z \Rightarrow z\,\,\text{is non real} 
\end{align}
Therefore the real part of \frac{1}{1 - z} is $0$.
